---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// type: "reel" uses /reel/{id}/embed/ and a 9:16 frame with chrome cropped.
// type: "post" uses /p/{id}/embed/ and a taller frame to fit the photo plus chrome.
const posts = [
  { id: "DXKTjK-RaCf", type: "reel" as const },
  { id: "DXcYo5viZgU", type: "reel" as const },
  { id: "DXuZu4wkfwi", type: "post" as const },
];
---

<Base title="Social Media | Grace Joy Park">
  <h1>Social Media</h1>
  <p class="muted lead">How I used Instagram this semester to cover Athens, build a beat audience and learn how working journalists publish in the feed.</p>

  <section class="reflection">
    <h2>My Instagram plan</h2>
    <p>
      I came into Reporting I planning to use Instagram the way working
      journalists do, as a place to publish field reels, build a small
      audience around my business beat and show editors I can think in
      pictures and pacing, not just inverted pyramids. I posted to
      @grace.park_uga across the semester and tagged @ugagrady on the
      reels that fit the Grady community feed.
    </p>

    <h2>What I posted and the skills I gained</h2>
    <p>
      The Beat Meet and Greet reel introduced me to my source, Cayce Reese
      of Oconee State Bank, and gave me practice interviewing on camera
      before I sat down for the longer Q&amp;A. Cutting a clip down to 10
      seconds taught me to pick a single sentence that carried the meeting,
      then let the room do the rest. The Athens Orthopedic Clinic Twilight
      Criterium reel pushed me into shooting a scene as it unfolded, the
      banner over Silver Dollar Bar, the food court setup, the cyclists
      scouting the course, and condense a race weekend into a clip without
      voiceover. The carousel post taught me that a single still can carry
      a story when the caption is written like a lede instead of a quote.
    </p>

    <h2>Engagement</h2>
    <p>
      The numbers were modest but real for a school-quarter beat. The Beat
      Meet and Greet reel earned 22 likes, the Twilight reel 12 and the
      event carousel 15, mostly from classmates, sources and Grady faculty.
      The lesson was that reach on a small account comes from tagging the
      people in the frame and the institutions covering them, not from
      chasing a trending audio. Tagging @ugagrady on the Twilight reel put
      it in front of the school&rsquo;s journalism community, which is a
      better audience for a clip than a viral one.
    </p>

    <h2>Accounts I followed and learned from</h2>
    <p>
      I followed @apnews and @nytimes for how they write the caption like
      a headline rather than a description of the photo. @ugagrady showed
      me the rhythm of how a journalism school presents student work, and
      gave me a model for how to write a reel caption that reads like a
      tip line. I also followed peers in my Reporting I cohort to track
      how they were covering their own beats, which kept me honest about
      pacing and consistency.
    </p>

    <h2>The opportunity for journalists on Instagram</h2>
    <p>
      Instagram rewards reporters who treat reels like field notebooks. A
      10-second clip from a city commission meeting or a race weekend can
      reach readers who would never click a 700-word story, and a tagged
      source often reshares the reel to an audience my byline never
      reaches. For a business beat in Athens, the opportunity is to build
      a recurring presence with the small businesses and community
      institutions I cover, so the next time I email asking for an
      interview, they recognize me from the feed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 class="posts-heading">Selected posts</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {posts.map(({ id, type }) => (
      <div class={`embed ${type === "reel" ? "is-reel" : "is-post"}`}>
        <iframe
          src={`https://www.instagram.com/${type === "reel" ? "reel" : "p"}/${id}/embed/`}
          title={type === "reel" ? "Instagram reel" : "Instagram post"}
          loading="lazy"
          scrolling="no"
          allow="autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; picture-in-picture; web-share"
          allowtransparency="true"
          allowfullscreen
        ></iframe>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .lead { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }

  .reflection {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .reflection h2 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .reflection h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .reflection p {
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .posts-heading {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(320px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: start;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .embed {
    width: 100%;
    /*
     * Cap each embed at the width Instagram's internal player actually
     * fills. Above ~330px the video stops scaling and IG fills the leftover
     * space with black side bars. Capping here removes the bars and keeps
     * the embed centered inside its grid column.
     */
    max-width: 330px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    background: #000;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    overflow: hidden;
    position: relative;
  }

  /*
   * Reels: 9:16 frame so the reel video fills top to bottom. Then oversize
   * the iframe and offset it upward so Instagram's username header and
   * likes/comments footer push past the visible edges.
   */
  .embed.is-reel {
    aspect-ratio: 9 / 16;
  }

  .embed.is-reel iframe {
    position: absolute;
    top: -60px;
    left: 0;
    width: 100% !important;
    height: calc(100% + 220px) !important;
    border: 0;
    display: block;
  }

  /*
   * Carousels / photo posts: photo inside is roughly square, so a 1:1
   * frame plus the chrome-crop trick shows just the image.
   */
  .embed.is-post {
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    background: #fff;
  }

  .embed.is-post iframe {
    position: absolute;
    top: -60px;
    left: 0;
    width: 100% !important;
    height: calc(100% + 220px) !important;
    border: 0;
    display: block;
  }
</style>
